extra condition is needed on A to ensure this, \deleted{as we may take for A
any full subcategory of (Cat)$_{\mathrm{as}}$ stable under products and containing ,
but not $\emptyset$ , which will ensure that A is \inserted[typed]{strict} a test category,} \xout{Sorryxxxx}
\xout{was going to add that Then and the inclusion functor} To see this, let's
take a finite group G, and an aspherical topological space $E_G$ upon
which G operates freely, with quotient $B_G$ , a classifying space of G.
I\over{s}{f} G $\neq$ 1\hand{,} the homotopy type of $B_G$ is'nt of finite type, because $B_G$ has
non vanishing cohomology groups in arbitrarily high dimensions, as
well known. We could transpose the \inserted{following} construction in either (Cat) or
\hand{$\Delta^{\wedge}$} say, \xout{h}but we may as \xout{q}well work in the modelizer \inserted{$M=$}(Spaces), and take
any \inserted{small} full subcategory A of M , containing the unit interval \deleted{but not}\inserted{but not}
\deleted{the empty space}\inserted{the empty space}, and stable under finite products - which implies
that A is a strict test category. We'll take A large enough to contain
$E_G$ \xout{as an object} , and small enough to be made up with aspheric spaces,
hence the inclusion functor
\[ i:\xout{f}A \longrightarrow \text{(Spaces)} \]
is a test functor. Now consider the quotient object \uline{in} A$^{\wedge}$
\[ F = E_G \,/\, G \quad \text{in } A^{\wedge} \quad , \]
i.e. the \xout{functorxxxxxAxxF}\inserted[typed]{presheaf} on A
\[ F: \quad T \longmapsto \mathrm{Hom}(T,E_G)/G \ \simeq\ \mathrm{Hom}(T,B_G) \qquad , \]
\xout{xxx}where the last isomorphism comes from the fact that the object T of A
is \xout{a}aspheric and hence 1-connected. Thus we get
\[ F \unsure{\simeq} i^{*}(B_G) \quad , \]
hence the homotopy type \xout{of} defined by F is the homotopy type of $B_G$ ,
which is not of finite type, despite the fact that F is of finite pre-%
sentation.

In order to insure that the homotopy type defined by any object
in $A^{\wedge}{}_{\mathrm{fp}}$ be of finite type, it may be useful perhaps to make \xout{d}on A the
assumption that any quotient in A$^{\wedge}$ of an obje\xout{c}ct in A is isomorphic to an
object in A, and that the set of all subobjects in A$^{\wedge}$ of an object \inserted{a} in
A (i.e. the set of all sieves on a ) is finite - possibly too that
for any two objects a and b of A, Hom(a,b) is finite. As for the
opposite inclusion, namely that any homotopy type of finite type can
be described by an object of $A^{\wedge}{}_{\mathrm{fp}}$ , this would follow from the existen-%
ce of a \uline{perfect} test functor from \hand{$\Delta$} into A$^{\wedge}$ , factoring through

