Th\unsure{e} description I just recalled of U(k) is the one most intuitive to
my mind,  other people may prefer the more computational one given on
page 451 in terms of $\Gamma_k\hat{}\,(M)$  (endowed with it's augmentation to k and it's
diagonal map),  which is of importance in it's own right.  It shows
the existence of a canonical k-\uline{linearization} \uline{functor}
\[ \text{(J)} \qquad L : U(k) \longrightarrow Ab_k \quad , \]
giving rise to the commutative diagram (up to can. isom.)
\deleted{\xout{$Ab_k$xxxxxx$W$xxxxxxx$Ab_k$xxW(k)}}
\diagram{
\text{(J')}\qquad
\begin{tikzcd}
Ab_k \arrow[rr, "W"] \arrow[dr, "\Gamma_k\hat{}"'] & & U(k) \arrow[dl, "L"] \\
& Ab_k &
\end{tikzcd}
\quad \xout{,}
}
where
\[ \Gamma_k\hat{}\,(M) = \hand{\prod}_{i\unsure{\geq}0} \Gamma_k^{\,i}(M) \qquad . \]
This linearization is not quite compatible with ring extension,  it beco-%
mes so only when we view it as a functor with values,  not just in the
category $Ab_k$ of k-modules,  but of separated and complete \xout{k-modules for}
linearly topologized k-modules,  the ring extension functor for these
being \uline{completed} tensor product.  This is a little (or big ?) technical
\margin{\unsure{x}} drawback for this notion of linearization.  We have a canonical embedding
\[ \text{(K)} \qquad \hand{x \mapsto \exp(x) :} \qquad X \longrightarrow \xout{\unsure{W(L(X))}xxx}\ W\hat{}\,(L(X)) \qquad \hand{\text{(cf. p.450, (n))}} \]
functorial in X,  \xout{which satisfies a} where for a topological k-module M
as above,  described as a filtering inverse limit of discrete ones $M_i$ ,
we define
\[ \text{(K')} \qquad \xout{W}\ W\hat{}\,(M) = (k' \longmapsto \underset{\hand{\leftarrow\, i}}{\underline{\lim}}\ M_{\unsure{i}} \otimes_k k' \ ) \quad . \]
The map (K) has a universal property with respect to all possible maps
$X \to W\hat{}\,(M)$  with M a linearly topologized separated and complete k-module,
which acounts for it's role as ``linearization''.  It should be noted here
that the map (C) on page (467) does'nt quite exist,  we have corrected
this point here -  definitely we cannot in (K) replace $W\hat{}$ by W .  Of cour-%
se linearization L (or it's variant $L_{pt}$)  does'nt commute in any sense
whatever to restriction of ground ring.

The image of  X  in $W\hat{}\,(L(X))$  is caracterized by the simple
formulae (q) p. 451 .  Maps from X to Y  may be described as just continu-%
ous \inserted{k-linear} maps from  L(X) to L(Y),  compatible with augmentations and diag\unsure{o}nal
maps.

