\[ P\otimes_k k' \quad ,\quad \text{for given homomorphism } k\to k' \ , \]
for instance as the solution of the obvious 2-universal problem \xout{of} \inserted{corresponding to}
mapping P k-additively into k'-additive categories, or more evidently
by taking the same objects as for P,  but with
\[ \mathrm{Hom}_{P'}(a,b) = \mathrm{Hom}_{P}(a,b)\otimes_k k' \quad . \]
Thus any ``coalgebra stucture in (Cat)'' ($\ast$) over the ground ring k,  \xout{give}
gives rise to similar structure over ground ring k'.

Of course,  among the relevant axioms for the diagonal functor ($\ast$),
is the existence of unit objects in $P^{\&}$ and $Q^{\&}$ ,  which may be viewed
equally as \inserted{k-additive} functors (defined up to unique isomorphism)
\[ P^{o}\to Ab_k \quad , \quad P\to Ab_k \qquad , \]
playing the role I would think of ``augmentation'' and ``coaugmentation''
in the more familiar set-up of \inserted{ordinary} coalgebras.  Denoting these objects by
$k_P$  and $k_Q$  respectively (in analogy to the constant presheaves $k_A$  and
$k_B$ on A and B) ,  \xout{Ext} $\mathrm{RHom}(k_P\ ,\ -\ )$  now allows expression of
cohomology or cointegration,  and  $-\overset{L}{\ast}_k(k_Q)$  allows expression of
homology or integration (for complexes in $P^{\&}_{\hand{\mathcal{M}}}$ say).  ``Constant coeffi-%
cients''  on P i.e. in $P^{\&}$ may now be defined,  as objects in $P^{\&}$ of the
type
\[ U\otimes_k k_P \qquad , \]
where U is in $Ab_k$  i.e. is any k-module,  and hence we get homology and
cohomology invariants with coefficients in any such U (or complexes of
such),  and surely too cup and cap products... Also,  quasi-isomorphisms
