\hand{2)} It is well known that an abelian topos \hand{$\mathcal{P}$} is equivalent to a catego-%
ry (k-Mod), for suitable ring k (not necessarily commutative) iff it \xout{admi}
admits an object L which is\hand{:}a) generating\hand{,} and b) ``\uline{ultraprojective}'',
i.e. the functor $X\mapsto \mathrm{Hom}(L,X)$ commutes to small direct\inserted{limits} \xout{limits}. The condi-%
tion b) (for an object of an \uline{abelian} category stable under small direct
limits or equivalently, under small direct sums) is equivalent with L
being: b$_1$) projective, and b$_2$) of``\uline{finite presentation}'' , i.e. the
functor $X\mapsto \mathrm{Hom}(L,X)$ commutes with \inserted{small} filtering direct limits. This obser-%
vation suggests one common feature of all the \inserted{examples of abelian} duality pairings considered
so far, namely that the abelian topoi under consideration in the pairing
\uline{have\xout{\unsure{s}} a small set of ultraprojective generators}. I do'nt know if a struc-%
ture theory of such categories\hand{,}\hand{(which} are the abelian analogs \xout{of} for topoi
equivalent to topoi of the special type A\^{}, \hand{(}with A in (Cat)) has been
worked out\xout{,} yet. I did'nt do it at any rate - but the natural thing to
expect is that these abelian topoi \hand{$\mathcal{P}$}(which we may call ``\uline{algebraic}'' ones,
just as\inserted{an} ordinary topos equivalent to one of the type A\^{} may be called
``algebraic'', which e qually means that the \inserted{set of} ultraprojective objects in it
\deleted{are}\inserted{is} generating ...) are exactly those equivalent to a category of the type
\[ \xout{\mathrm{Hom}(P,(Ab))}\ \ \underline{\mathrm{Hom\ add}}\ (P^{\circ}, (Ab)) \quad , \]
\margin{\uline{Hom} $\mathbb{Z}$}
where P is any \uline{small} \uline{additive} category, and where \uline{Hom add} denotes
the category of additive functors from one additive category to another.
Instead of assuming P small, we may as well take P merely``essentially
small'' i.e. equivalent to a small category, with the benefit that for
a given \hand{$\mathcal{P}$} , there is a canonical choice of an\xout{x} additive category P
together with an equivalence
\[ \hand{\mathcal{P}}\ \hand{\xrightarrow{\approx}}\ \underline{\mathrm{Hom\ add}}\ (P^{\circ}, (Ab)) \ , \]
namely by taking
\[ P = \text{full subcategory of } \hand{\mathcal{P}} \text{ made up with all ultraprojective}\xout{s} \]
\[ \text{objects in } \hand{\mathcal{P}}. \]

